# What never enters a shipped image. Mirror of the rule: the service source is
# what runs, tests/ is what checks, dev-process/ is how we work.
#
# Read against the REPOSITORY ROOT context, which is where the root Dockerfile
# builds the three Python services from. The Next app has its own context and
# its own web/.dockerignore; this file says nothing about it.
#
# The bare and the `**/` spelling of a name are BOTH listed wherever the name
# occurs at more than one depth. A docker ignore pattern is anchored at the
# context root, so `tests` excludes ./tests and nothing else -- and every test
# tree in this repository is nested (packages/helpdesk/tests, services/*/tests),
# which `COPY packages/ services/` would otherwise carry straight into the
# image. Pinned by packages/helpdesk/tests/test_container_build.py::
# test_the_dockerignore_excludes_every_private_path.

# version control and editor
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.editorconfig
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store

# secrets and local config (real config is injected at runtime)
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.cache

# the coding agent and its tooling
.claude
CLAUDE.md
.mcp.json

# verification, CI, and dev tooling
tests
**/tests
project-evals
infra
.github
.pre-commit-config.yaml
.gitleaks.toml
.trivyignore
Makefile

# our working process and documentation
dev-process
docs
README.md
LICENSE

# build artefacts and caches: the image installs its own
.venv
**/.venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
.mypy_cache
**/.pytest_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.mypy_cache

# the Next app: it is built from web/ by its own Dockerfile, never from here
web

# the policy corpus: it arrives as a read-only runtime mount, never baked in,
# so the corpus that decides access is not pinned to a build
policies

# the images' own recipes
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# private working files (never in an image either)
notes.md
his.md
test-mode.md
REQUIREMENTS.md
pl
